# Skill Invocation Telemetry (`aifirst event`)

When you start running one of this project's skills, record it to the aifirst
usage log with `aifirst event`. This makes skill usage observable without
changing what the skill does. Record **only** this single start event — do not
emit completion, failure, or correlated follow-up events.

## When this applies

Apply to **every `af-*` skill** (the ones whose name begins `af-nso-` or
`af-qa-`, for example `af-nso-preflight`, `af-nso-spec-orchestrator`,
`af-nso-manage-package`, `af-qa-strategy`). Record **once per invocation**, at
the start, before doing the skill's work. When a skill delegates to another
skill, the delegated skill records its own start event.

## What to run

At the start, before doing the skill's work, record the invocation:

```bash
aifirst event skill.invoked --attr skill=<skill-name> --attr agent=<agent>
```

- `<skill-name>` — the `name` from the skill's `SKILL.md` frontmatter
  (e.g. `af-nso-preflight`).
- `<agent>` — the coding agent you are running as (`copilot`, `cursor`, or
  `claude`).

Fire it once and move on — do not read, wait on, or act on its output.

## Rules

- **Best-effort, never blocking.** If `aifirst` is not on `PATH` or the command
  errors, continue the skill silently — telemetry must never fail, delay, or
  change the outcome of the user's task. Do not install anything to make it work.
- **Fire-and-forget.** Record the single `skill.invoked` event and proceed
  immediately; do not capture a trace id, wait for a result, or emit a matching
  completion/failure event.
- **No secrets in `--attr`.** Only record non-sensitive metadata (skill name and
  agent). The CLI masks secret-looking keys, but never pass credentials, tokens,
  API keys, or other secrets regardless.
- **Keep it quiet.** Recording telemetry is routine plumbing — run it in the
  background of your normal work and keep your response focused on the user's
  request.
